Gibbs Sampling: One Conditional at a Time

Group: Bayesian Statistics & Posterior Inference

An interactive, standalone HTML/CSS/JavaScript demonstration of systematic-scan
Gibbs sampling. No Shiny, WebR, installation, CDN, or runtime download is needed.
Open index.html directly, or serve this directory with any static web server.
The multivariate Gaussian and Student-t examples use a single
two-dimensional observation, known scale matrix, and a flat location prior.
A Poisson likelihood with a Gamma-Gamma hierarchy demonstrates positive,
non-Gaussian posterior parameters. Every update uses an exact full conditional.
The two-population Gaussian mixture adds adjustable separation and population
weight, with shared within-population scales and correlation. Its full
conditionals are Gaussian mixtures whose weights are recomputed from the held
coordinate, not fixed at the global population weights. The mixture parameters
are specified, not inferred; this is not a latent-class parameter-fitting model.
The numerical kernels and target definitions are separate from the interface:
gibbs.js implements seeded sampling and distributions; app.js handles Canvas
plots, controls, animation, and diagnostics. Additional families can implement
the same target/conditional interface. styles.css supplies responsive styling.
The numerical primitives now live in ../TeachingTools/statistics.js, shared
with the other browser-native inference tools. Keep TeachingTools/ beside
Gibbs/ when copying the source for offline use.

Play or pause individual coordinate updates, step a coordinate or sweep,
jump to an update, rewind, or display the complete chain. The joint trajectory
and full-conditional density are synchronised. Playback begins paused.

Two coordinate updates make one complete sweep. Only complete sweeps already
revealed by playback are used in trace plots, marginal histograms, sample
summaries, and autocorrelation plots. Burn-in never changes the underlying
chain. The seed fixes the trajectory, so playback and display changes do not
resample it. Changing a model or sampling setting generates a new chain and
rewinds playback. Student-t scales are not standard deviations; Gamma
parameters use rates rather than scales.

Compare overlapping and separated mixture populations to see mode trapping:
exact Gibbs updates need not explore both populations within the displayed run.
The mixture marginal overlays include both populations even when the chain
visits only one. Compare different starts and seeds; burn-in alone does not
solve poor mixing.

Run JavaScript numerical and stationary-distribution tests (Node 22 or later):
  node --test Gibbs/test_gibbs.js

Run actual browser interaction, playback, plotting, responsive-layout, and
standalone-file tests (Chrome required; CHROME_BIN can select its executable):
  node Gibbs/test_browser.js

The browser runner uses Node built-ins only, a temporary isolated Chrome
profile, and a loopback-only static server. It closes both when finished.
Tests also check that the page requests no external or Shiny/WebR assets.

Build the HTML tools for GitHub Pages:
  node deploy.js

The html-tools.json manifest copies index.html, styles.css, gibbs.js, and
app.js into _site/Gibbs, and statistics.js into _site/TeachingTools.
The clean Pages artifact contains only native HTML tools and shared assets.
All registered tools are native HTML; retained R references are not published.

The previous app.R and gibbs.R are retained as an R/Shiny numerical reference,
not loaded by the HTML tool or copied into its Pages directory. Their existing
regressions can still be run with Rscript Gibbs/test_gibbs.R. JavaScript and R
use different pseudorandom generators; matching seeds do not match paths.

Teaching reference:
https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section3c_html.html#building-an-mcmc-sampler-the-gibbs-sampler
